          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.083,
            0.61,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": " Weigh each cup using a balance (Fig. 6.7) and record the readings in your notebook."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.13,
            0.611,
            0.172
          ],
          "content": "Compare the data and infer which is heavier or lighter."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.177,
            0.611,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": "From Activity 6.8, we can say that any object which is heavier or lighter can be measured in terms of a property called **mass**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.239,
            0.611,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": "The one which is heavier has more mass and the one which is lighter has less mass."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.681,
            0.083,
            0.792,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.635,
                0.273,
                0.848,
                0.307
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.7: Weighing a paper cup containing water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.329,
            0.723,
            0.388
          ],
          "content": "**Weight is sometimes used in common language for mass as it is determined by weighing. You will learn more about mass and weight and their relation in higher classes.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.414,
            0.396,
            0.437
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.3.6 Space and volume"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.448,
            0.746,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "Next day, Madam Vidya enters the class. All the students stand up to greet her. She reciprocates and deliberately says, “Please keep your bags on your seats and sit down.” Students are not able to sit because bags are kept on their seats. Madam Vidya asks, “Why are you not sitting?” The students reply that there is no place to sit because the bags have occupied that space."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.597,
            0.462,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": "Continuing the conversation, she provides two identical glass tumblers to two students and encourages them to pour the remaining water from their drinking water bottles into the respective glass tumblers. On pouring water in the glass tumblers, the students observe that one glass tumbler gets halffilled with water (Fig. 6.8a) while the other is almost completely filled with water (Fig. 6.8b)."
        },
        {
